      "raw_html": "<div class=\"question\" id=\"question-75300\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">2.</span><a class=\"questionKP\" href=\"/topics/3854#631\">Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve</a><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"75300\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>Calculate <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"336\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mstyle><mjx-mfrac><mjx-frac type=\"d\"><mjx-num><mjx-nstrut type=\"d\"></mjx-nstrut><mjx-mrow><mjx-msup><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c64\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.421em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D466 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-mrow></mjx-num><mjx-dbox><mjx-dtable><mjx-line type=\"d\"></mjx-line><mjx-row><mjx-den><mjx-dstrut type=\"d\"></mjx-dstrut><mjx-mrow><mjx-mtext class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c64\"></mjx-c></mjx-mtext><mjx-msup><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D465 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: 0.289em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msup></mjx-mrow></mjx-den></mjx-row></mjx-dtable></mjx-dbox></mjx-frac></mjx-mfrac></mjx-mstyle></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mtext>d</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mtext>d</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> for the polar curve <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"337\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 119.4%; position: relative;\" tabindex=\"0\"><mjx-math aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"MJX-TEX\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D45F TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"4\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c63\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c73\"></mjx-c><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c63\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2061\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D703 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2E\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml display=\"inline\" unselectable=\"on\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>csc</mi><mo data-mjx-texclass=\"NONE\">⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>.</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container> </p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyModerate\">M</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Wed, Oct 14th @ 11:28 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 1:27</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "question-331821",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $B(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-1)t}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t=B(s-1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-1>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-331821-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
