      "dom_id": "question-334221",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
